00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:47.330
Like, any bottoms?
比如说，有穿下半身的衣服吗？

352
00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:49.390
I mean, I had on, like, undies.
我是说，我穿了内衣什么的。

353
00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:50.010
You had undies.
你穿了内衣。

354
00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:52.430
But I didn't have on pants.
但我没穿裤子。

355
00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:53.110
That's a weird look.
这造型真够怪的。

356
00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:54.910
So I had on this, like, long,
所以我穿了件这种，挺长的，

357
00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:56.950
it was kind of like an Oxford shirt.
有点像那种牛津纺衬衫。

358
00:12:57.210 --> 00:12:57.510
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
对，对，对。

359
00:12:57.510 --> 00:13:00.550
So it hung down even past my underwear.
所以它下垂得甚至盖过了我的内衣。

360
00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:02.050
Oh, this is very 80s.
哦，这太八十年代风格了。

361
00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:07.070
No, I was like Tom Cruise in Risky Business,
不，我当时就像《乖仔也疯狂》里的汤姆·克鲁斯一样，

362
00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:10.470
just walking out in my big Oxford with no pants on.
就穿着我的大牛津衬衫没穿裤子走出来。

363
00:13:10.630 --> 00:13:13.250
And then I hid in my house for the next four years
然后我在家里躲了整整四年，

364
00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:14.510
and never spoke to him again.
再也没跟他说过话。

365
00:13:14.630 --> 00:13:14.970
Oh, boy.
天哪。

366
00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:15.790
I was so embarrassed.
我太尴尬了。

367
00:13:16.030 --> 00:13:18.910
Those are some major snafus, you guys.
这些可都是些大失误啊，各位。

368
00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:22.390
That's, yeah, that's big stuff.
对，没错，这是大事。

369
00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:24.990
This one might be bigger.
这个可能更大。

370
00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:26.210
I hope so.
但愿如此。

371
00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:27.730
Angela and Jenna,
安吉拉和珍娜，

372
00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:31.950
are you ready to dive into today's snafu?
你们准备好深入了解今天的糗事了吗？

373
00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:34.190
I'm so excited.
我太兴奋了。

374
00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:35.090
So excited.
超级兴奋。

375
00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:36.550
I'm having a sip of my iced tea.
我抿了一口冰茶。

376
00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:37.710
I'm settling in.
我正舒舒服服地坐好。

377
00:13:37.830 --> 00:13:38.230
Get ready.
准备好。

378
00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:38.970
Get your popcorn.
准备好爆米花。

379
00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:41.090
I love a snafu.
我可太喜欢糗事了。

380
00:13:41.270 --> 00:13:41.750
I love you.
我爱你。

381
00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:42.710
I love this pod.
我爱这个播客。

382
00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:46.050
So three of my favorite things are about to happen all at once.
所以我最喜欢的三件事马上就要同时发生了。

383
00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:46.310
Awesome.
太棒了。

384
00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:47.870
And I love Angela, my BFF.
而且我爱安吉拉，我的好闺蜜。

385
00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:48.350
There you go.
这就对了。

386
00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:48.950
So four things.
所以是四件事。

387
00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:51.010
This is just an abundance of riches right here.
这简直是好上加好、应有尽有。

388
00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:51.570
It is.
确实如此。

389
00:13:55.590 --> 00:14:00.030
Today's snafu takes us aboard the USS Ticonderoga,
今天的糗事把我们带到了“提康德罗加号”航空母舰上，

390
00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:04.910
a massive aircraft carrier stationed off the coast of Japan in 1965.
这是一艘1965年驻扎在日本海岸附近的庞大航空母舰。

391
00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:09.810
This thing was built like it was auditioning for a Cold War action movie.
这东西造得就像是在为冷战动作片试镜一样。

392
00:14:09.950 --> 00:14:14.710
Tough exterior, fighter jets everywhere, total mid-century badass.
坚固的外表，到处都是战斗机，绝对是上世纪中叶的硬汉风格。

393
00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:19.190
Picture if Don Draper and Maverick from Top Gun had a baby,
想象一下，如果唐·德雷珀和《壮志凌云》里的独行侠生了个孩子，

394
00:14:19.370 --> 00:14:21.210
and the baby was a boat.
而这个孩子是一艘船。

395
00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:24.710
And it, like, chain-smoked all the time.
而且它还整天烟不离手。

396
00:14:24.850 --> 00:14:28.110
Like, that's what this aircraft carrier was like.
真的，这艘航母就是这副德行。

397
00:14:28.230 --> 00:14:30.210
Pure American military swagger.
纯正的美式军事派头。

398
00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:35.710
Unfortunately, that swagger was about to trip into a colossal face plant.
不幸的是，这种派头马上就要变成一次惨烈的当众出丑了。

399
00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:42.910
So what happened was what is known in military circles as a broken arrow incident.
所以发生的事情在军事界被称为“断箭”事件。

400
00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:44.850
Do either of you know what that term means?
你们两个知道这个词是什么意思吗？

401
00:14:45.970 --> 00:14:48.390
There was a movie called Broken Arrow?
曾经有一部电影叫《断箭》吗？

402
00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:49.350
Yes, there was.
是的，有过。

403
00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:50.130
I don't.
我不记得了。

404
00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:51.770
Do you know what the movie was about?
你知道那部电影讲的是什么吗？

405
00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:52.910
It might get, it might help.
可能会吧，可能会有帮助。

406
00:14:56.290 --> 00:14:56.690
Okay.
好吧。

407
00:14:56.950 --> 00:14:58.670
I feel like a guy jumped off a train.
我觉得里面有个家伙从火车上跳下来了。

408
00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:00.590
Maybe that happened in that movie.
也许那部电影里发生过这事。

409
00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:07.630
But a broken arrow incident is, that's when we lose a nuclear device.
但“断箭”事件是指我们丢失了核装置。

410
00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:09.410
Oh, my goodness.
哦，天哪。

411
00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:09.690
Which has happened.
而且这种情况确实发生过。

412
00:15:09.710 --> 00:15:11.850
I think the nuclear device was on the train.
我觉得那个核装置就在火车上。

413
00:15:12.030 --> 00:15:12.210
Yeah.
是啊。

414
00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:13.150
Okay, yeah.
好吧，对。

415
00:15:13.790 --> 00:15:14.550
Okay, whatever.
好吧，随便吧。

416
00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:16.110
Let go, let it go, Angela.
放手吧，安吉拉，别纠结了。

417
00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:17.790
I feel like John Travolta was in that movie.
我觉得约翰·特拉沃尔塔演过那部电影。

418
00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:19.510
I can't remember anything else about it.
其他关于它的事我什么也记不起来了。

419
00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:28.150
But anyway, the term broken arrow, that refers to any time we lose a nuclear missile or a bomb or device of some sort.
但无论如何，“断箭”这个术语指的是我们丢失核导弹、炸弹或某种装置的任何时候。

420
00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:34.750
And it happens, it has happened throughout history, numerous, like way more than you want to know.
而且这种事确实会发生，历史上发生过很多次，多到远远超乎你的想象。

421
00:15:35.330 --> 00:15:38.410
I mean, enough times they had to have a code name for it.
我的意思是，发生的次数多到他们不得不为此起一个代号。

422
00:15:38.410 --> 00:15:41.850
Well, losing a nuclear weapon sounds completely insane.
嗯，弄丢核武器听起来简直太疯狂了。

423
00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:42.890
And it is.
确实如此。

424
00:15:43.030 --> 00:15:50.150
But part of why I wanted to bring this story to you guys in particular is that it has kind of a familiar flavor.
但我之所以想把这个故事讲给你们听，部分原因在于它有一种似曾相识的味道。

425
00:15:50.390 --> 00:15:54.850
Clashing personalities, baffling miscommunication, questionable leadership choices.
性格冲突、令人费解的沟通不畅、可疑的领导决策。

426
00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:58.570
Basically, this is a classic workplace comedy.
基本上，这就是一部经典的职场喜剧。

427
00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:01.970
This is if Michael Scott was in charge.
这就好像迈克尔·斯科特当家作主一样。

428
00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:02.890
Yeah, yeah.
对，对。

429
00:16:03.070 --> 00:16:06.930
And instead of cubicles, we're on a thousand-foot warship in the middle of the Pacific Ocean.
而且我们不是在格子间里，而是在太平洋正中央一艘千英尺长的军舰上。

430
00:16:07.130 --> 00:16:09.470
Instead of paper jams, we're dealing with hydrogen bombs.
我们处理的不是卡纸，而是氢弹。

431
00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:14.510
Attention all new and existing members of the WDNC.
请注意，所有WDNC的新老会员。

432
00:16:14.750 --> 00:16:18.330
I'm Melanie Sanders, and this is the We Do Not Care Club podcaSt.
我是梅兰妮·桑德斯，这里是“我们毫不在乎俱乐部”播客。

433
00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:22.590
We got to midlife and realized we just simply do not care much anymore.
我们到了中年才意识到，我们真的再也懒得在乎太多事情了。

434
00:16:22.850 --> 00:16:30.670
Now, we got to help each other make sense of hot flashes, itchy ears, chin hair, trifling families, brain fog, and bat wings.
现在，我们得互相帮助，去应对潮热、耳朵发痒、下巴长毛、讨嫌的亲戚、脑雾和拜拜肉。

435
00:16:30.890 --> 00:16:33.550
We are not alone, and we will be heard.
我们并不孤单，我们的声音会被听到。

436
00:16:33.550 --> 00:16:35.450
So come join the WDNC.
所以快来加入WDNC吧。

437
00:16:35.650 --> 00:16:40.830
Follow and listen to the We Do Not Care Club podcast with Melanie Sanders wherever you get your podcaSt.
在各大播客平台关注并收听由梅兰妮·桑德斯主持的“我们毫不在乎俱乐部”播客。

438
00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:49.570
On December 5th, 1965, just off the coast of Japan's Ryukyu Islands,
1965年12月5日，就在日本琉球群岛海岸附近，

439
00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:50.050
U.S.
美国

440
00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:56.490
naval officers were running through a routine training exercise called the Crew Cut on the USS Ticonderoga.
海军军官正在“提康德罗加”号航空母舰上进行一项名为“船员精简”的例行训练演习。

441
00:16:56.890 --> 00:17:04.790
An A-4E Skyhawk, an attack fighter jet, was being wheeled into the elevator to be taken up to the flight deck.
一架A-4E“天鹰”攻击战斗机正被推上升降机，准备运往飞行甲板。

442
00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:06.310
So quick pause.
所以我们先暂停一下。

443
00:17:07.170 --> 00:17:08.910
Are you guys nervous, Flyers?
你们会紧张吗，飞行员们？

444
00:17:09.110 --> 00:17:17.950
I am not, but I will say that for some reason the idea of landing or taking off on an aircraft carrier scares the bejesus out of me.
我不紧张，但我得说，不知为什么，在航空母舰上降落或起飞的想法吓得我魂飞魄散。

445
00:17:18.050 --> 00:17:30.290
It's like I don't know if it's the tiny runway or just the fact that it's surrounded by water or that it's constantly moving or that there's like 30 people on deck just casually waving around glow sticks like it's a rave for fighter jets.
就好像——我不知道是因为那条极短的跑道，还是因为它四周环水，或者是因为它一直在移动，又或者是甲板上有30个人在随性挥舞着荧光棒，搞得就像是战斗机专属的锐舞派对一样。

446
00:17:30.290 --> 00:17:33.650
I just, this is very un-chill vibes for me.
总之，这让我感觉非常没有安全感。

447
00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:41.430
Also, like with the short runway, don't they have like a cable that catches the plane and jerks it back?
还有，跑道那么短，他们是不是有一根用来勾住飞机并猛然将其拽停的阻拦索？

448
00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:45.970
I mean, if you need a cable to catch your plane on a landing, I don't know.
我的意思是，如果降落时需要一根缆绳来勾住飞机，我可说不准。

449
00:17:46.090 --> 00:17:46.990
What if you miss the cable?
要是没勾住缆绳怎么办？

450
00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:49.570
I got invited to do something like that.
我曾被邀请去做类似的事情。

451
00:17:49.570 --> 00:17:57.450
There was like, they invited me to be like in a cockpit for like a special flying mission thing.
就好像是，他们邀请我去，比如说什么特殊飞行任务的驾驶舱里。

452
00:17:57.590 --> 00:18:00.550
And I was like, I don't need to read anymore.
然后我就想，我不用再往下读了。

453
00:18:00.550 --> 00:18:03.110
Like, this is not going to go well for anyone.
这绝对对谁都没什么好结果。

454
00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:06.610
You're just going to have a lady throwing up on everyone.
只会变成一个女人在所有人身上狂吐不止。

455
00:18:07.830 --> 00:18:17.660
For this crew cut exercise, the pilot, Lieutenant Douglas Webster, was simply supposed to take off, fly around for a bit, and then land again,
对于这次的演练，飞行员道格拉斯·韦伯中尉本来只需要起飞、飞一会儿，然后再降落，

456
00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:20.600
a routine drill to practice loading and unloading cargo.
一个练习装卸货物的例行演练。

457
00:18:20.800 --> 00:18:24.240
But almost immediately, things went off the rails.
但几乎立刻，情况就失控了。

458
00:18:24.440 --> 00:18:29.580
As the Skyhawk was being positioned on the elevator, it suddenly began rolling backward.
当“天鹰”战机被推到升降机上时，它突然开始往后滑行。

459
00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:34.380
The crew on the deck started waving frantically, shouting for Webster to hit the brakes.
甲板上的船员们开始疯狂挥手，大喊着让韦伯踩刹车。

460
00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:41.860
A few even ran behind the aircraft, trying to stop it from rolling, heroic, but not exactly realistic.
甚至有几个人跑到了飞机后面，试图阻止它后退，这虽然很英勇，但完全不切实际。

461
00:18:42.300 --> 00:18:49.600
The Skyhawk was one of the Navy's lighter planes, but it still had a takeoff weight of 24,500 pounds.
“天鹰”是海军较轻的飞机之一，但它的起飞重量仍然达到了24500磅。

462
00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:52.280
So this was not a rogue shopping cart.
所以这可不是一辆失控的购物车。

463
00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:55.640
This is like a tank with wings, basically.
这基本上就像是一辆带翅膀的坦克。

464
00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:00.380
Somehow, Webster didn't catch on to all the chaos going on around him.
不知怎么的，韦伯竟然没有察觉到周围发生的这一切混乱。

465
00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:09.900
And in a split second, the Skyhawk rolled off the side of the carrier, tore through the safety netting, and vanished into the ocean.
就在转瞬之间，“天鹰”冲出了航母侧舷，撞破安全网，消失在大海之中。

466
00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:18.420
What would you do if you're a crew member on this flight deck and you're just, like, standing there watching this?
如果你是这个飞行甲板上的船员，就这么站在那里看着这一幕，你会怎么做？

467
00:19:19.100 --> 00:19:20.700
Expletives, some cursing.
各种脏话，骂骂咧咧。

468
00:19:20.920 --> 00:19:22.120
Who's getting fired?
谁要被炒鱿鱼了？

469
00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:23.160
Someone's getting fired.
肯定有人要被炒了。

470
00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:23.900
Yeah, for sure.
是的，肯定的。

471
00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:26.940
I would be like, we got to tell somebody.
我会觉得，我们得赶快告诉别人。

472
00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:33.360
Have you ever, like, gone to sit down on the toilet and your phone's in your pocket and then it falls in the toilet?
你有没有过这种经历，想坐到马桶上，结果手机还在口袋里，然后它就掉进马桶了？

473
00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:34.220
Oh, my God, no.
噢，天哪，没有。

474
00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:36.240
That's just, like, the ultimate version of that.
这简直就是那种情况的终极版。

475
00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:38.320
That's never happened to me, thank God.
谢天谢地，我从来没遇到过。

476
00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:41.260
That's never – you've never dropped your phone in the toilet?
从来没有——你从来没把手机掉进过马桶里？

477
00:19:41.260 --> 00:19:42.040
No, I never have.
没有，我从来没有。

478
00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:42.720
Oh, my gosh.
噢，天哪。

479
00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:44.120
I've done this before.
我以前干过这事。

480
00:19:44.420 --> 00:19:48.140
Or dropped your phone in a swimming pool or something, in a pool of water.
或者把手机掉进游泳池什么的，掉进水池里。

481
00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:49.080
Well, but that's different than a toilet.
好吧，但那跟马桶可不一样。

482
00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:49.900
That's what this is reminding me of.
这就是这事让我想到的地方。

483
00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:57.140
Okay, but every time I go into an airport bath, like, the urinals in an airport, I'm just, like, I pocket my phone for that exact reason.
好吧，但每次我进机场洗手间，比如机场的小便池，我就会因为这个原因把手机揣得紧紧的。

484
00:19:57.160 --> 00:20:02.040
I'm so – because if, like, my phone goes into, like, a public urinal, it's staying there.
我太——因为如果我的手机掉进公共小便池里，它就得留在那里了。

485
00:20:02.060 --> 00:20:03.680
Like, no, I ain't getting it out.
不可能，我才不会把它捞出来呢。

486
00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:05.080
It is done.
它彻底完蛋了。

487
00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:06.560
Goodbye, phone.
再见吧，手机。

488
00:20:07.060 --> 00:20:12.120
I wonder if it was – because, like, when it falls into the ocean, like, it's gone.
我在想，是不是因为——就像它掉进大海里一样，就没了。

489
00:20:12.120 --> 00:20:23.340
Like, it ain't – you can't see anything, and I just wonder if it went through anyone's head to just pull one of those, like – like, the crew on the deck is, like,
就像它不是——你什么也看不见，我只是在想，有没有人脑子里闪过这样一个念头——就像甲板上的船员那样，

490
00:20:23.360 --> 00:20:24.660
where's the plane?
飞机呢？

491
00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:26.320
Somebody's, like, what plane?
有人会说，什么飞机？

492
00:20:26.460 --> 00:20:26.800
What are you talking about?
你在说什么呢？

493
00:20:27.080 --> 00:20:27.260
What?
什么？

494
00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:27.840
Hmm?
嗯？

495
00:20:28.020 --> 00:20:28.360
Plane?
飞机？

496
00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:29.180
There was no plane.
根本没有飞机。

497
00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:29.340
What?
什么？

498
00:20:29.660 --> 00:20:29.980
Don't know.
不知道。

499
00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:30.660
There was no plane?
根本没有飞机？

500
00:20:30.660 --> 00:20:31.140
I'm not sure.
我不太确定。

501
00:20:31.520 --> 00:20:32.340
There's other planes.
还有别的飞机。

502
00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:32.680
You know what?
你知道吗？

503
00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:33.780
But there was no plane here.
但这里根本没有飞机。

504
00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:35.460
Here's the other plane.
这不就是另一架飞机吗。

505
00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:36.460
How dare you?
你怎么敢这样？

506
00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:39.560
The confident –
那个自信的——

507
00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:40.760
How dare you ask me that?
你怎么好意思问我这个？

508
00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:43.820
You just got to come in with, like, full – the confident rebuttal.
你一进来就得带着那种——充满自信的反驳。

509
00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:44.100
What?
什么？

510
00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:45.160
How dare you?
你怎么敢？

511
00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:47.340
There was a plane here two minutes ago.
两分钟前这里明明有一架飞机。

512
00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:48.200
No, there – what?
不，这里——什么？

513
00:20:49.300 --> 00:20:50.440
I would know.
我会知道的。

514
00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:52.040
I was standing here.
我就站在这里。

515
00:20:52.040 --> 00:20:53.520
My job was to stop it from rolling.
我的工作就是阻止它滑动。

516
00:20:54.980 --> 00:21:00.600
So, the crew sprang into action, calling nearby ships to launch a full search and rescue operation,
于是，机组人员立即行动起来，呼叫附近的船只展开全面的搜救行动，

517
00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:07.220
but it wasn't just about finding the beloved Lieutenant Douglas Webster, who was still inside the plane, as it's saying.
但这不仅仅是为了找到备受爱戴的道格拉斯·韦伯斯特中尉，正如所说，他当时还在飞机里。

518
00:21:07.580 --> 00:21:08.240
Oh, my God.
哦，天哪。

519
00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:09.220
I didn't realize that.
我之前没意识到这一点。

520
00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:09.680
Yeah, yeah.
是啊，是啊。

521
00:21:09.900 --> 00:21:10.220
Ed!
埃德！

522
00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:11.120
There's a guy in the plane.
飞机里有人。

523
00:21:11.260 --> 00:21:13.440
We didn't know if he was still in the plane.
我们当时不知道他是不是还在飞机里。

524
00:21:13.440 --> 00:21:13.920
He was in the plane.
他在飞机里。

525
00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:15.860
I thought he was – oh.
我以为他——哦。

526
00:21:16.120 --> 00:21:16.780
Oh, no.
哦，不。

527
00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:17.430
He was in the plane.
他在飞机里。

528
00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:17.760
Yeah.
是啊。

529
00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:19.220
Oh, no.
哦，不。

530
00:21:19.260 --> 00:21:19.560
I know.
我知道。

531
00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:20.780
Spoiler alert.
剧透警告。

532
00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:22.440
He did not make it.
他没能活下来。

533
00:21:22.680 --> 00:21:23.040
Oh.
哦。

534
00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:24.500
He was not recovered.
他的遗体没有被找到。

535
00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:25.580
That's terrible.
太可怕了。

536
00:21:25.760 --> 00:21:26.020
I know.
我知道。

537
00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:29.240
Is he the only – was he the only crew member and the person in the plane?
他是唯一的——他不仅是唯一的机组人员，还是飞机里唯一的人吗？

538
00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:29.620
Yes.
是的。

539
00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:31.200
He's the only one who perished.
他是唯一遇难的人。

540
00:21:31.600 --> 00:21:31.650
Oh, my goodness.
哦，天哪。

541
00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:33.580
There was also something else on board.
机上还有别的东西。

542
00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:33.630
There was also something else on board.
机上还有别的东西。

543
00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:37.240
A one-megaton nuclear bomb.
一枚百万吨级的核弹。

544
00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:38.140
Yeah.
是啊。

545
00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:46.160
For reference, that's 70 times more powerful than the bomb dropped on Hiroshima, which, to put it in Dunder Mifflin terms,
作为参考，这比投在广岛的原子弹威力大70倍，用邓德米弗林公司的术语来解释，

546
00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:52.740
is like someone misplacing a really important post-it note, if that post-it note could also vaporize a city.
这就好比有人弄丢了一张非常重要的便利贴，前提是这张便利贴也能瞬间蒸发一座城市。

547
00:21:52.740 --> 00:22:03.240
So, this whole enormous plane with the super-duper nuke and this poor man just go over the edge.
所以，这整架装着超级核弹的巨大飞机和这个可怜的男人就这样冲出了边缘。

548
00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:03.560
Yes.
是的。

549
00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:06.620
They go – they just go right over the edge.
他们就——他们直接冲出了边缘。

550
00:22:07.800 --> 00:22:10.920
And I love the term you just said, super-duper nuke.
我挺喜欢你刚才用的那个词，超级核弹。

551
00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:14.600
It feels like a –
感觉就像是——

552
00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:16.580
I think that – I believe that's the technical term.
我觉得——我相信那是专业术语。

553
00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:16.780
Yeah.
对。

554
00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:19.420
Do you guys load the super-duper nuke?
你们会装填那个超级无敌核弹吗？

555
00:22:19.520 --> 00:22:20.100
Oh, yeah.
哦，对。

556
00:22:20.460 --> 00:22:21.300
It's ready to go.
随时准备发射。

557
00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:27.900
So, a frantic search followed, but tragically, neither the pilot nor the aircraft was ever recovered.
于是，接下来是一场疯狂的搜救，但不幸的是，无论是飞行员还是飞机都再也没有被找到。

558
00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:34.480
The only thing they found was his helmet bobbing alone on the surface of the ocean, which really sends chills down your spine.
他们唯一找到的只有他独自漂浮在海面上的头盔，这真的让人不寒而栗。

559
00:22:34.660 --> 00:22:35.760
So, here's the thing.
所以，事情是这样的。

560
00:22:35.860 --> 00:22:39.980
This was not just a casual cruise that the Ticonderoga was on.
提康德罗加号当时执行的绝不仅仅是一次随意的巡航。

561
00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:48.160
It had just wrapped up combat duty in Vietnam and was en route to Japan for some much-needed rest and refueling.
它刚结束在越南的战斗任务，正前往日本进行急需的休整和补给。

562
00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:52.720
And that's when the inevitable cover-up begins.
然后，不可避免的掩盖行动就开始了。

563
00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:55.260
So, let's break down the optics for a second.
那么，我们来分析一下当时的舆论视觉效果。

564
00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:55.820
The U.S.
这艘美国

565
00:22:55.920 --> 00:23:08.100
aircraft carrier, fresh from war, accidentally drops a nuclear bomb just off the coast of Japan, a country with, let's say, a very specific set of feelings about nuclear bombs.
航空母舰刚从战场归来，就在日本海岸线附近不小心丢了一枚核弹，而日本这个国家对核弹可以说是怀有一种非常特殊的感情。

566
00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:12.760
This is – again, it's 1965, so not too much time has passed.
而且——再强调一次，那是1965年，所以时间并没有过去多久。

567
00:23:12.760 --> 00:23:20.880
Oh, and just for some added detail, we had signed a treaty promising we wouldn't bring nukes anywhere near Japanese shores.
哦，为了增加点细节，我们当时还签署了一项条约，承诺不会把核武器带到日本海岸附近的任何地方。

568
00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:24.380
So, yeah, this was not going to go over well.
所以，是的，这绝对会引起轩然大波。

569
00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:29.380
Was the ship more than 200 nautical miles off the coastline of Japan?
这艘船当时距离日本海岸线超过200海里吗？

570
00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:29.920
Oh, my God.
哦，天哪。

571
00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:30.480
Who are you?
你是什么人？

572
00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:31.080
What kind of –
这算是什么——

573
00:23:31.080 --> 00:23:33.060
This is a very specific question.
这是一个非常具体的问题。

574
00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:44.420
Well, I had just read that – I had just read that every country is responsible and has ownership of the first 200 nautical miles off their shoreline.
嗯，我刚好读到过——我刚读到说每个国家对离岸前200海里的海域都负有责任并拥有管辖权。

575
00:23:45.170 --> 00:23:52.940
So, if this was within Japan's 200 nautical miles, that is a very big breach because you're not in open ocean.
所以，如果这发生在日本的200海里以内，那就是一个非常严重的违规，因为你当时不在公海上。

576
00:23:53.180 --> 00:23:53.320
Yeah.
对。

577
00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:57.460
I believe it was well within Japanese jurisdiction.
我认为这完全在日本的管辖范围内。

578
00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:01.780
It was a very bad look.
这观感非常糟糕。

579
00:24:02.420 --> 00:24:08.480
And so, of course, to avoid the bad look, the Navy tried to cover it up.
因此，理所当然地，为了避免难看，海军试图掩盖真相。

580
00:24:09.160 --> 00:24:11.100
They zipped it up, tight-lipped.
他们守口如瓶，封锁了消息。

581
00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:17.320
But as we all know, military secrets have a way of slinking into the public zeitgeist eventually.
但众所周知，军事秘密总有一天会悄悄流传到公众的视野中。

582
00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:19.620
Are you guys good secret keepers?
你们几个擅长保守秘密吗？

583
00:24:19.760 --> 00:24:20.300
Are you trustworthy?
你们值得信任吗？

584
00:24:20.800 --> 00:24:21.500
Lock it up.
守口如瓶。

585
00:24:21.660 --> 00:24:22.400
Lock it up.
守口如瓶。

586
00:24:22.580 --> 00:24:22.820
Yeah.
对。

587
00:24:23.020 --> 00:24:26.300
But not – this is not a secret I could keep.
但不是——这个秘密我可保不住。

588
00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:26.820
Yeah.
对。

589
00:24:26.820 --> 00:24:28.300
I would be a whistleblower.
我会去告密。

590
00:24:28.480 --> 00:24:33.660
I'm good at keeping your secrets or Angela's secrets or friends and family secrets.
我很擅长保守你们的秘密，或者安吉拉的秘密，又或者是朋友和家人的秘密。

591
00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:34.980
My own secrets.
我自己的秘密也行。

592
00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:43.700
But I'm not going to keep your we-lost-a-nuclear-weapon-in-the-ocean secret for you if you're the military.
但如果你是军方，我们把核武器弄丢在海里这种秘密，我可不会帮你们保守。

593
00:24:43.700 --> 00:24:48.340
Like, I'm going to be the lady who is writing a report on that.
我会成为那个写相关调查报告的女士。

594
00:24:55.000 --> 00:25:02.440
The Navy was spectacularly or perhaps frighteningly able to conceal this incident for over 15 years.
海军极其出色，或者说令人毛骨悚然地将这起事件隐瞒了15年之久。

595
00:25:02.440 --> 00:25:06.020
It wasn't until 1981 that the Pentagon disclosed the incident.
直到1981年，五角大楼才披露了这起事件。

596
00:25:06.220 --> 00:25:12.460
The Washington Post reported about it in a mere three sentences buried in a nuclear accidents report.
《华盛顿邮报》对此进行了报道，但仅仅把它埋在一份核事故报告的三句话里。

597
00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:19.280
However, the folks at the Pentagon also did not bother explaining the details of where exactly the bomb was.
然而，五角大楼的人也没有费心去解释这枚炸弹具体在哪里的细节。

598
00:25:19.280 --> 00:25:23.980
They first stated that the bomb was more than 500 miles from land.
他们最初声称炸弹距离陆地有500多英里。

599
00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:25.260
Vague much?
还能再含糊一点吗？

600
00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:32.820
They also assured everyone that the bomb posed no danger since it was 16,000 feet underwater.
他们还向所有人保证，炸弹不会构成任何危险，因为它在水下16,000英尺深的地方。

601
00:25:32.820 --> 00:25:43.500
But in 1989, the environmental group Greenpeace, alongside naval expert William Arkin, exposed more naval documents,
但在1989年，环保组织绿色和平与海军专家威廉·阿金公开了更多海军文件，

602
00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:52.640
revealing that the bomb was dropped only 70 miles off the coast of the Ryuku Islands, as opposed to the 500 they had said before.
揭露这枚炸弹实际上是投在距离琉球群岛海岸仅70英里处，而不是他们之前声称的500英里。

603
00:25:52.640 --> 00:26:04.020
They also pointed out that this incident violated Japan's strict anti-nuclear policies and exposed some serious Navy secrets about nuclear weapons in Vietnam.
他们还指出，这一事件违反了日本严格的无核政策，并暴露了一些关于美国在越南部署核武器的严重海军机密。

604
00:26:04.460 --> 00:26:12.960
With no other choice, the Pentagon finally admitted the whole truth about this gigantic snafu in a statement that same year in 1989.
五角大楼别无选择，终于在1989年同年发表声明，承认了这一惊天大乌龙的全部真相。

605
00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:20.120
Did this create a diplomatic issue for the United States and the Japanese government?
这给美国和日本政府制造了外交危机吗？

606
00:26:20.640 --> 00:26:21.040
Yeah.
对。

607
00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:22.080
In 1989?
在1989年？

608
00:26:22.620 --> 00:26:23.360
Yeah, for sure.
对，肯定的。

609
00:26:23.500 --> 00:26:33.400
It feels like these international incidents are—there's always some measure of just sort of posturing of, like, we have to respond this way because protocol demands it of us,
感觉这些国际事件——总有一种装模作样的成分，比如，我们必须这样回应，因为规程要求我们这么做，

610
00:26:33.400 --> 00:26:39.380
and so we must make these statements, and we must say these things, and we must say that we're outraged, and then the U.S.
所以我们必须发表这些声明，我们必须说这些话，我们必须表示我们感到愤怒，然后美国

611
00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:45.920
must respond with their, like, diplomatic explanation and acceptance of responsibility.
必须用他们那种外交解释和接受责任的方式来进行回应。

612
00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:48.600
It's like, it's so choreographed, all these things.
这感觉就像，所有这些事情都是排练好的。

613
00:26:48.600 --> 00:26:50.460
It's very wild how it all works out.
这一切运作的方式真的很疯狂。

614
00:26:50.460 --> 00:26:53.540
And then I always wonder, like, does anyone actually take this personally?
然后我总是想，会有人真的把这当作个人恩怨吗？

615
00:26:54.100 --> 00:26:57.820
If, like, Japan is expressing outrage, like, how dare they?
如果像日本在表达愤怒，比如，他们怎么敢？

616
00:26:58.100 --> 00:27:03.520
Is there any, like, Japanese official who's, like, genuinely pissed, like, at a personal level?
有没有哪个日本官员，是那种真的被激怒了，比如在个人层面上的？

617
00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:07.060
Or is it just sort of, like, grand institutional emotion?
还是说这只是一种体制上的情绪表演？

618
00:27:07.340 --> 00:27:13.160
I'm also curious, what happens to a nuclear—a super dupe nuke?
我也很好奇，核武器——超级无敌核弹会怎么样？

619
00:27:13.400 --> 00:27:14.300
Super duper nuke?
超级无敌核弹？

620
00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:15.660
A super duper nuke.
一颗超级无敌核弹。

621
00:27:15.660 --> 00:27:17.160
Does it get pressurized?
它会被加压吗？

622
00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:19.320
How deep was the water 70 miles off?
70英里外那里的水有多深？

623
00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:20.300
This is a great question.
这是一个很好的问题。

624
00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:22.500
And Greenpeace had a lot to say about this.
绿色和平组织对此有很多话要说。

625
00:27:22.660 --> 00:27:23.000
Okay.
好的。

626
00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:31.480
So when the Pentagon admitted this in 1989, they were actually responding to an article in Newsweek, and then Japanese papers picked up the story, which, of course,
所以当五角大楼在1989年承认这一点时，他们实际上是在回应《新闻周刊》的一篇文章，接着日本报纸也跟进报道了此事，理所当然地

627
00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:35.480
caused quite a stir throughout Japan itself, rightfully so.
在日本国内引起了轩然大波。

628
00:27:35.480 --> 00:27:40.500
Though the Pentagon confirmed that the bomb would not be harmful—it hadn't been armed,
尽管五角大楼确认该炸弹不会造成危害——它并未被引信武装，

629
00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:47.540
and you have to go through a whole bevy of procedural elements to actually arm it—it was still 16,000 feet underwater, decaying,
而且你必须通过一整套繁琐的程序才能真正将其武装——但它仍然沉在16000英尺的水下并且正在腐烂，

630
00:27:48.100 --> 00:27:54.060
which meant it might eventually break down and start to emit serious pollutants.
这意味着它最终可能会破损并开始散发严重的污染物。

631
00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:55.160
Yet, U.S.
然而，美国

632
00:27:55.340 --> 00:27:59.360
spokespersons claimed this wouldn't cause any harm to the natural world.
发言人声称这不会对自然界造成任何损害。

633
00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:08.520
Either way, it was safe to say they were in the hot seat, at least as hot as the nuclear material inside that bomb, shall we say.
